    level: "C1",
    text: "Measurements and units are essential for describing the physical world with precision. Length is measured in millimetres, centimetres, metres, and kilometres in the metric system, or inches, feet, yards, and miles in the imperial system. Weight uses grams, kilograms, and tonnes (metric) or ounces, pounds, and stones (imperial). Volume is measured in millilitres and litres (metric) or fluid ounces, pints, and gallons (imperial). The UK uses a confusing mix of both systems: road distances are in miles, height is often in feet and inches, weight can be in stones and pounds, but food is sold in grams and kilograms. Temperature is measured in degrees Celsius (°C) or Fahrenheit (°F). Speed combines distance and time: kilometres per hour (km/h) or miles per hour (mph). Area is measured in square metres (m²) or square feet (ft²), and land in hectares or acres. Time units range from seconds and minutes to hours, days, weeks, and years. Understanding how to express measurements in English — including the prepositions and vocabulary around them — is vital for cooking, travel, shopping, science, construction, and countless everyday situations.",
